# Alfis in a container

Prebuilt multi-arch images (amd64 + arm64) are published to GHCR:

    docker pull ghcr.io/revertron/alfis:latest

Run a resolver node (DNS on 53, P2P on 4244), persisting data in ./cache:

    docker run -d --name alfis --net=host -v $(pwd)/cache:/var/lib/alfis ghcr.io/revertron/alfis:latest

Or publish only the ports you need instead of --net=host:

    docker run -d --name alfis \
      -p 53:53/udp -p 53:53/tcp -p 4244:4244 \
      -v $(pwd)/cache:/var/lib/alfis \
      ghcr.io/revertron/alfis:latest

Provide your own config by mounting it over /etc/alfis.conf. To build the image
locally from source instead of pulling, run `make build` in this directory.

## Web UI

The image includes Web UI support, but it remains disabled unless Alfis is
started with `--web-ui` and a non-empty password is configured. Copy
`contrib/docker/alfis.toml`, uncomment its `[webui]` section, and replace the
example password. Keep `listen = "0.0.0.0:8082"`: this makes the service
reachable through Docker's port mapping, while the host-side `127.0.0.1`
binding below prevents direct LAN access.

    docker run -d --name alfis \
      -p 127.0.0.1:8082:8082 \
      -p 4244:4244 \
      -v $(pwd)/cache:/var/lib/alfis \
      -v $(pwd)/alfis.toml:/etc/alfis.conf:ro \
      ghcr.io/revertron/alfis:latest \
      -n --web-ui -c /etc/alfis.conf

Access it from another machine through an SSH tunnel:

    ssh -L 8082:127.0.0.1:8082 user@server

Then open http://127.0.0.1:8082 in a browser. The Web UI uses plain HTTP, so do
not publish port 8082 on all host interfaces unless a TLS reverse proxy and
appropriate access controls are in place.
